# Phase 18 POP/Lite POP header parity slice — 2026-10-18T11:18:55Z

- Branch: `phase/19-phase-18-compatibility-closure`; base revision `1b032468`.
  Java oracle remains pinned to RocketMQ client 5.5.1 / source marker
  `efa1be59d6fc747ad220f036c86030f05f0419f9`; no Java checkout changes.
- Source/bytecode inspection confirmed `PopLiteMessageRequestHeader` uses
  reflection decoding, has a no-op `checkFields()`, and extends
  `RpcRequestHeader`. Nullable client/group/topic/attempt strings remain null;
  missing or malformed primitive numbers stay at zero; inherited `ns`, `nsd`,
  `bname`, and `oway` are serialized and participate in base equality/hash.
  Added the header to the request-header identity oracle (42 classes total).
- Added Java fixtures for both decode hints, absent/malformed/overflow values,
  valid Arabic-Indic numerals, base fields, and `isTimeoutTooMuch()` elapsed and
  far-future cases. Rust now preserves nullable strings and inherited fields,
  matches Java's base-only equality/hash, and exposes the timeout helper for
  Lite POP. The same Java timeout helper is now mapped for regular POP. Added
  boundary and signed-overflow regressions.
- First Protocol all-feature run caught an invalid `compile_fail` doctest that
  expected `PopLiteMessageRequestHeader` not to implement equality. The class
  inherits equality from Java `RpcRequestHeader`, so the incorrect guard was
  removed and the complete Protocol test run then passed.
